Actually, the issue is that `word8_add3` writes all 8 elements of `z`. But then `x[i+j] = CT::select(mask, z[j], x[i+j])` reads `z[j]` for all 8. So `z` is fully written by `word8_add3`. Hmm.

Wait, but the crash is in `bigint_cnd_abs`, not `bigint_cnd_add/sub`. Let me re-read. The crash state is:
```
Botan::word_add
Botan::bigint_cnd_abs
Botan::ct_inverse_mod_odd_modulus
```

So `word_add` is called from `bigint_cnd_abs`. In `bigint_cnd_abs`:
```cpp
word carry = mask & 1;
for(size_t i = 0; i != size; ++i)
   {
   const word z = word_add(~x[i], 0, &carry);
   x[i] = CT::select(mask, z, x[i]);
   }
```

`word_add(~x[i], 0, &carry)` reads `x[i]`. So `x[i]` = `a_w[i]` is uninitialized. This means `a_w` has uninitialized words.

So the question is: why would `a_w` have uninitialized words? `a` is grown to `mod_words`. Let me check `grow_to` again:

```cpp
void BigInt::grow_to(size_t n)
   {
   if(n > size())
      {
      if(n <= m_reg.capacity())
         m_reg.resize(m_reg.capacity());
      else
         m_reg.resize(round_up(n, 8));
      }
   }
```

`m_reg.resize(m_reg.capacity())` — resize to capacity. `secure_vector::resize` should zero-initialize new elements. But wait — does `secure_vector` zero-initialize on resize? Let me check.

Actually, the key insight: `a` is `n` (the input x). If `n` is 0, `a` has 0 words. `a.grow_to(mod_words)` resizes. But what if `n` is NOT 0 but has fewer words? E.g., `n` has 1 word, `mod` has 2 words. `a.grow_to(2)` resizes to 2 words. The second word is zero-initialized. Fine.

